<node name="/" xmlns:doc="http://www.freedesktop.org/dbus/1.0/doc.dtd">
  <!--
      org.freedesktop.portal.experimental.Certificate:
      @short_description: Portal for using certificates and keys held on a security token

      This interface lets applications use a certificate and its private key
      without ever seeing the private key, and without talking to PKCS#11
      modules, card readers or PIN entry themselves. The application asks for
      a certificate that fits a purpose, the user picks one and authorizes its
      use, and the application can then ask for signatures made with the
      matching key.

      The token is usually a smart card reached through a PKCS#11 module - a
      PIV card, a national eID card, a hardware security key - but it may
      equally be a TPM or a software key store. This interface names the
      credential rather than the hardware: what crosses it is a certificate
      and operations on the key behind it, never a PKCS#11 object, session or
      token, and never a card reader.

      The private key never crosses this interface. Neither does the PIN: the
      backend is responsible for any PIN entry, and no key material, PIN value
      or PIN source ever appears in an argument or a result.

      A grant is a session.
      :ref:`org.freedesktop.portal.experimental.Certificate.CreateSession`
      creates an empty one,
      :ref:`org.freedesktop.portal.experimental.Certificate.AcquireCredential`
      fills it in with a certificate the user chose, and
      :ref:`org.freedesktop.portal.Session.Close` ends it. A session holds at
      most one credential for its whole life: a second
      ``AcquireCredential`` on a session that already acquired one is
      refused, and acquiring again means creating another session and asking
      the user again. A grant is bound to the D-Bus connection that created
      it, to the operations and mechanisms the frontend allowed, and it
      always expires.

      Expiry is the portal's. When a grant reaches the end of its lifetime
      the portal emits
      :ref:`org.freedesktop.portal.experimental.Certificate::GrantInvalidated`
      with ``reason`` ``expired`` and closes the session, so an application
      is told rather than finding out at its next
      :ref:`org.freedesktop.portal.experimental.Certificate.Sign`. A backend
      that expires the same grant on its own clock says so with its own
      signal, and whichever arrives first, the application is told once.

      This is an **experimental** interface. It is not exported unless
      ``XDG_DESKTOP_PORTAL_ENABLE_EXPERIMENTAL`` contains ``certificate``, and
      it can change or get removed without a version bump.

      This documentation describes version 1 of this interface.
  -->
  <interface name="org.freedesktop.portal.experimental.Certificate">
    <!--
        CreateSession:
        @options: Vardict with optional further information
        @handle: Object path for the :ref:`org.freedesktop.portal.Request` object representing this call

        Creates a grant session. The session starts out empty; call
        :ref:`org.freedesktop.portal.experimental.Certificate.AcquireCredential`
        on it to have the user select a certificate and authorize its use.

        Supported keys in the @options vardict include:

        * ``handle_token`` (``s``)

          A string that will be used as the last element of the @handle. Must be a valid
          object path element. See the :ref:`org.freedesktop.portal.Request` documentation for
          more information about the @handle.

        * ``session_handle_token`` (``s``)

          A string that will be used as the last element of the session handle. Must be a
          valid object path element. See the :ref:`org.freedesktop.portal.Session`
          documentation for more information about the session handle.

        The following results get returned via the :ref:`org.freedesktop.portal.Request::Response` signal:

        * ``session_handle`` (``o``)

          The session handle. An object path for the
          :ref:`org.freedesktop.portal.Session` object representing the created
          session.

        A non-zero response carries an optional ``reason`` (``s``): the portal
        emits ``session_closed`` when the session was closed before it could
        be handed over, and otherwise forwards the backend's own.
    -->
    <method name="CreateSession">
      <annotation name="org.qtproject.QtDBus.QtTypeName.In0" value="QVariantMap"/>
      <arg type="a{sv}" name="options" direction="in"/>
      <arg type="o" name="handle" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!--
        AcquireCredential:
        @session_handle: Object path for the :ref:`org.freedesktop.portal.Session` object
        @parent_window: Identifier for the application window, see :doc:`window-identifiers`
        @options: Vardict with optional further information
        @handle: Object path for the :ref:`org.freedesktop.portal.Request` object representing this call

        Asks the user to select a certificate and authorize its use for the
        given purpose. On success the session named by @session_handle becomes
        a grant, and
        :ref:`org.freedesktop.portal.experimental.Certificate.Sign` can be
        called on it.

        A session acquires at most once. If the session already acquired a
        credential, or an acquisition on it is still in flight, the response
        is ``2`` with ``reason`` ``grant_already_held`` and nothing is asked
        of the user.

        The vardicts below are closed vocabularies: a key this version does
        not know is an error rather than a value that is quietly dropped,
        because a filter or a policy that is silently ignored offers the user
        more than the application asked for.

        Supported keys in the @options vardict include:

        * ``handle_token`` (``s``)

          A string that will be used as the last element of the @handle. Must be a valid
          object path element. See the :ref:`org.freedesktop.portal.Request` documentation for
          more information about the @handle.

        * ``activation_token`` (``s``)

          A token that can be used to activate the chooser window, see
          :doc:`window-identifiers`.

        * ``purpose`` (``s``)

          Required. What the credential is for. One of ``client_auth``,
          ``signing``, ``email`` or ``ssh``. There is deliberately no value
          meaning "anything": the purpose selects the consent wording, narrows
          the certificates the user is offered, and bounds the operations the
          grant may be used for. In this version every purpose bounds them to
          ``sign``.

        * ``certificate_filter`` (``a{sv}``)

          Narrows the certificates offered to the user. All keys are optional
          and are combined with a logical AND. The keys are ``issuers``
          (``aay``, DER encoded issuer distinguished names), ``key_usage``
          (``as``), ``eku`` (``as``, extended key usage OIDs),
          ``key_algorithms`` (``as``), ``token_label`` (``s``) and
          ``piv_slot`` (``s``, one of ``authentication``, ``signature``,
          ``key_management`` or ``card_authentication``).

          A filter narrows what is offered; it never widens it, and it is not
          a security boundary.

        * ``operation_policy`` (``a{sv}``)

          The operations the application is asking for. ``sign`` (``b``) is
          the only key this version defines, and an operation the policy does
          not mention is asked for, so the default is ``{'sign': true}``. A
          policy that asks for nothing is an error, not an empty grant.

          This is where a later version asks for an operation which is not
          signing, and it is a closed vocabulary now so that asking for one
          this version does not have is an error rather than a grant that
          silently does not carry it.

        * ``requested_lifetime`` (``u``)

          How long, in seconds, the application would like the grant to live.
          This is a ceiling request, not a floor: the portal may grant less and
          never grants more than its own maximum, which is advertised as
          ``max_grant_lifetime`` by
          :ref:`org.freedesktop.portal.experimental.Certificate.GetCapabilities`.
          There is no renewal in this version; a grant that has expired is
          replaced by a new session and a new consent.

        * ``interaction_mode`` (``s``)

          One of ``required`` (always prompt), ``allowed`` (prompt if needed,
          the default) or ``forbidden`` (never prompt; a request that would
          need a chooser or a PIN fails instead). The portal validates the
          value and forwards it; the backend owns the windows and is the end
          that honours it.

        * ``reason`` (``s``)

          A string that can be shown in the dialog to explain why the
          credential is needed. This is application-supplied text; the backend
          must present it as such and never in the position where it shows the
          identity of the application.

        The following results get returned via the :ref:`org.freedesktop.portal.Request::Response` signal:

        * ``certificate_der`` (``ay``)

          The selected leaf certificate, DER encoded. Always present when the
          response is ``0``.

        * ``chain_der`` (``aay``)

          Ordered intermediate certificates, best effort.

        * ``chain_status`` (``s``)

          How complete ``chain_der`` is: ``complete``, ``partial`` or
          ``leaf_only``. This describes completeness, not trust.

        * ``token_display`` (``a{sv}``)

          Token and reader identity for display only.

        * ``key_type`` (``s``)

          ``RSA`` or ``EC``.

        * ``key_size`` (``u``)

          Modulus size in bits, or curve size.

        * ``key_curve`` (``s``)

          The curve name, for EC keys.

        * ``supported_mechanisms`` (``as``)

          The mechanisms this grant may be used with, never empty. This is the
          portal's allow list intersected with what the backend reported.

        * ``permitted_operations`` (``as``)

          What this grant may be used for, never empty. It is what the backend
          reported, intersected with what ``operation_policy`` asked for and
          with what ``purpose`` allows, so it is never wider than the request
          the user was shown.

        * ``expires_at`` (``t``)

          When the grant expires, in seconds since the Unix epoch. This can be
          sooner than ``requested_lifetime`` asked for. It is the wall clock
          reading of the deadline taken at the moment of acquisition, and it
          is for display: the portal enforces the lifetime on a monotonic
          clock, so moving the system clock afterwards changes neither the
          grant nor this number, and the two stop agreeing.

        * ``may_prompt_later`` (``b``)

          Whether a later ``Sign`` on this grant may show a window.

        A non-zero response carries an optional ``reason`` (``s``): the portal
        emits ``grant_already_held`` for a second acquisition, ``grant_gone``
        when the session was closed while the user was deciding,
        ``backend_protocol_error`` when the backend's answer does not satisfy
        this interface - a required result missing or mistyped, or a grant
        that covers nothing that was asked for - and otherwise forwards the
        backend's own.
    -->
    <method name="AcquireCredential">
      <arg type="o" name="session_handle" direction="in"/>
      <arg type="s" name="parent_window" direction="in"/>
      <annotation name="org.qtproject.QtDBus.QtTypeName.In2" value="QVariantMap"/>
      <arg type="a{sv}" name="options" direction="in"/>
      <arg type="o" name="handle" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!--
        Sign:
        @session_handle: Object path for the :ref:`org.freedesktop.portal.Session` object
        @parent_window: Identifier for the application window, see :doc:`window-identifiers`
        @options: Vardict with optional further information
        @handle: Object path for the :ref:`org.freedesktop.portal.Request` object representing this call

        Signs a digest with the key of the certificate this grant is for.

        This is a prehashed signing API: the caller hashes the message and
        passes the digest, and the backend enforces the encoding the mechanism
        requires. Naming the hash and requiring ``data`` to be that hash's
        length is what makes the mechanism encoding well defined; it says
        nothing about where the digest came from.

        Supported keys in the @options vardict include:

        * ``handle_token`` (``s``)

          A string that will be used as the last element of the @handle. Must be a valid
          object path element. See the :ref:`org.freedesktop.portal.Request` documentation for
          more information about the @handle.

        * ``operation_id`` (``s``)

          An application-chosen string, returned unchanged in the results, so
          that a result and a diagnostic can be correlated without correlating
          them by content. It is not forwarded to the backend.

        * ``mechanism`` (``s``)

          Required. One of the names the grant reported in
          ``supported_mechanisms``: ``RSA_PKCS1_V1_5``, ``RSA_PSS`` or
          ``ECDSA``.

        * ``parameters`` (``a{sv}``)

          Whatever the mechanism needs, as a closed vocabulary:

          * ``hash`` (``s``)

            Required. Which digest ``data`` is. One of ``SHA1``, ``SHA224``,
            ``SHA256``, ``SHA384`` or ``SHA512``; the ``SHA-256`` spelling is
            accepted too.

          * ``mgf`` (``s``)

            ``RSA_PSS`` only. The mask generation function, spelled
            ``MGF1-<hash>`` - for example ``MGF1-SHA256``. Plain ``MGF1``
            means MGF1 over ``hash``, which is also the default when the key
            is absent.

          * ``salt_length`` (``u``)

            ``RSA_PSS`` only. The salt length in bytes, defaulting to the
            digest's length. The backend checks it against the modulus, which
            is the only place the modulus size is known.

          * ``signature_encoding`` (``s``)

            ``ECDSA`` only. ``raw`` (the default): the signature is r and s
            concatenated, each padded to the byte length of the curve order,
            which is what PKCS#11 produces. ``der``: the signature is the
            ``Ecdsa-Sig-Value`` SEQUENCE of RFC 3279.

        * ``data`` (``ay``)

          Required. The digest of the message under the ``hash`` named in
          ``parameters``, and never the message itself. Its length must be
          exactly that digest's length.

        The following results get returned via the :ref:`org.freedesktop.portal.Request::Response` signal:

        * ``signature`` (``ay``)

          The signature.

        * ``operation_id`` (``s``)

          The ``operation_id`` from the options, unchanged.

        A grant that does not exist, has already expired, or does not permit
        the requested mechanism is a D-Bus error from this method, before a
        :ref:`org.freedesktop.portal.Request` is handed back. The same
        condition reached after that - the session closed, or the grant
        expired, while the call was in flight - is a response of ``2`` with
        ``reason`` ``grant_gone``, because by then there is a Request to
        answer.

        A non-zero response carries an optional ``reason`` (``s``): the portal
        emits ``grant_gone`` as above and ``backend_protocol_error`` when the
        backend answered ``0`` without a signature.
    -->
    <method name="Sign">
      <arg type="o" name="session_handle" direction="in"/>
      <arg type="s" name="parent_window" direction="in"/>
      <annotation name="org.qtproject.QtDBus.QtTypeName.In2" value="QVariantMap"/>
      <arg type="a{sv}" name="options" direction="in"/>
      <arg type="o" name="handle" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!--
        GetCapabilities:
        @options: Vardict with optional further information
        @capabilities: Vardict describing what this portal and its backend can do

        Reports what this portal can do, so that an application can adapt
        without provoking a dialog. No window is shown and nothing is
        authorized by this call.

        The @options vardict is currently unused.

        The @capabilities vardict contains:

        * ``purposes`` (``as``)

          The purposes this portal accepts.

        * ``operations`` (``as``)

          What a grant can be used for.

        * ``mechanisms`` (``as``)

          The signature mechanisms this portal allows.

        * ``protected_authentication_path`` (``b``)

          Whether the backend can use a PIN pad on the reader instead of an
          on-screen PIN entry.

        * ``has_display`` (``b``)

          Whether the backend has anywhere to show a window. When it is
          false, an ``interaction_mode`` of ``required`` cannot be honoured
          and a request that would need a chooser or a PIN will fail.

        * ``max_grant_lifetime`` (``u``)

          The longest grant lifetime, in seconds, this portal will hand out.
    -->
    <method name="GetCapabilities">
      <annotation name="org.qtproject.QtDBus.QtTypeName.In0" value="QVariantMap"/>
      <arg type="a{sv}" name="options" direction="in"/>
      <annotation name="org.qtproject.QtDBus.QtTypeName.Out0" value="QVariantMap"/>
      <arg type="a{sv}" name="capabilities" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!--
        GrantInvalidated:
        @session_handle: Object path of the session whose grant is gone
        @reason: Why the grant is gone

        Emitted when a grant stops being usable before it was released. The
        session is closed as well; this signal exists to say why. A session
        which never acquired a credential has no grant to invalidate, so it
        is closed without this signal.

        It is emitted to the connection that owns the session and to no one
        else: a session object path carries the owner's unique bus name, so a
        broadcast would tell every application on the bus which peers hold
        certificate grants and when they end.

        @reason is one of ``expired``, ``token_removed``, ``policy``,
        ``backend_gone`` or ``error``. ``expired`` is the portal's own, and
        is emitted when the grant reaches the deadline it was given.
        Consumers must tolerate values they do not know.
    -->
    <signal name="GrantInvalidated">
      <arg type="o" name="session_handle"/>
      <arg type="s" name="reason"/>
    </signal>

    <property name="version" type="u" access="read"/>
  </interface>
</node>
